# The build context is the repository root, because the client's demonstration scenario
# imports a world fixture from `campaign/shared/test/fixtures`. Everything the image does
# not need is kept out here — a smaller context is a faster build, and secrets that are
# never sent to the daemon cannot end up in a layer.

# Python. The image runs the campaign server; `worldgen` generates the maps it is played
# on, and that happens on somebody's machine long before this is built.
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
htmlcov/
.coverage
worldgen/
tests/
scripts/
docs/
*.md

# Node artefacts. `node_modules` especially: it is reinstalled from the lock file inside
# the image, and copying a host one in would ship whatever platform it was built for.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
dist/
**/dist/
# Both spellings. A bare `*.tsbuildinfo` matches only the context root, so a stale
# `shared/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo` from a developer's machine travelled into the image and
# `tsc --build` decided the project was already up to date — emitting nothing, and leaving
# the client's build to fail resolving a package whose `dist/` did not exist.
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo
coverage/

# Somebody's actual campaign, and the journals beside it.
campaign.db*
**/campaign.db*

# Local noise.
.git/
.github/
.claude/
.idea/
.vscode/
*.log
